# The Node/npm floor is declared in package.json "engines" (node >=22 <23,
# npm >=10) and .nvmrc (22). CI (.github/workflows) and every Dockerfile stage
# build on node:22-alpine, so 22 is the authoritative toolchain.
#
# `engine-strict=true` USED TO BE HERE, and the reasoning was right: without it
# npm downgrades an engine mismatch to an EBADENGINE warning that scrolls past
# in install output, so a local Node 23/24 install silently produces a tree that
# was never validated against the version CI and production actually run.
#
# It is gone because it could not tell WHO was installing (#2230). On
# 2026-10-23 Dependabot's updater image moved to node 24.22.1 / npm 12.2.1, so
# `corepack npm@12 install --package-lock-only` became a hard `notsup` error
# against our engines range and EVERY security-update job failed — silently,
# because `Dependabot Updates` is GitHub-native and `ci-failure-issue.yml` has
# no workflow file to watch. A setting in this file is read by every npm on
# earth, including third-party runners whose node version is not ours to pick.
#
# The guarantee moved to where it is actually reachable:
#
#   · `.husky/pre-commit` runs `scripts/check-node-version.mjs` — LOCAL, which
#     is the only place a wrong-node tree can be produced, since every CI job
#     installs through `.github/actions/setup-node-prisma` and that pins
#     `node-version`. Dependabot never runs a git hook.
#   · that same action sets `npm_config_engine_strict` on its install step, as
#     belt and braces: a pinned node version is a claim about configuration,
#     and engine-strict is a check on what is running.
#
# `tests/guards/engine-strict-is-ci-only.test.ts` holds both halves, and holds
# that this file does NOT reintroduce the setting.
